# Trivy misconfig exceptions. Every entry needs a written reason in a comment above
# it — an unexplained line here is how a real finding becomes invisible.
#
# This file can only silence a rule id, repository-wide: that is all the plain-text
# format supports. The `trivy config` gate in .github/workflows/sec-iac.yml therefore
# uses trivyignore.yaml instead, whose entries are pinned to a path — needed there
# because that scan renders the vendored subchart too, and a rule tolerated for
# upstream's DaemonSet would stop flagging our own manifests. The two files overlap on
# KSV-0114 and must keep agreeing; the reasoning for it is written out below and
# referenced, not duplicated, from the YAML file.

# KSV-0114: "ClusterRole should not have access to mutatingwebhookconfigurations /
# validatingwebhookconfigurations for verbs [create update patch delete ...]".
#
# Two roles trip it and both need a write verb to function:
#   - cpu-pinning-operator-manager-role holds `patch` on the release's own
#     mutatingwebhookconfiguration, pinned with resourceNames, because the
#     BalloonPinningPolicy and BalloonsClusterConfig reconcilers rewrite the webhook's
#     namespaceSelector and failurePolicy at runtime. Its get/list/watch on the
#     resource are unscoped (the readiness-CA reconciler watches through the cache,
#     and list/watch cannot be restricted by name) but are read-only.
#   - cpu-pinning-operator-pre-delete holds `delete` on the release's own
#     validatingwebhookconfiguration so uninstall does not leave a webhook behind
#     that rejects every request against a controller that no longer exists.
#
# Two things this check does NOT respond to, both measured:
#   - Swapping the verb does not help. `patch` fires just as `update` does,
#     because its forbidden list is
#     [create update patch delete deletecollection impersonate *] — every verb that
#     can write. Only dropping write access entirely would satisfy it, and then the
#     reconcilers cannot do their job.
#   - Scoping does not help either. Both grants are restricted with resourceNames to
#     one named object and both are still flagged, so the rule keys on the
#     resource+verb pair alone.
#
# Accepted risk. Both write grants are already as narrow as the function allows (one
# verb, one named object each); do not reopen this entry expecting a verb or
# resourceNames change to clear it.
AVD-KSV-0114
KSV0114
KSV-0114
